 "source_type": "instagram",
 "source_url": "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DebLZmxSZSF/",
 "title": "Three Levels of Storytelling",
 "topic": "content creation",
 "summary": "The creator demonstrates three ways to improve storytelling by showing bad, OK, and great versions of the same story. The main lesson is to use specific details, raise stakes, and connect events using 'but' or 'therefore' instead of 'and then' to build tension.",
 "key_points": [
  "Get specific by adding little details that put the audience in the moment (what you saw, heard, felt, smelt, tasted).",
  "Raise the stakes by highlighting what you wanted or what you stood to lose.",
  "Connect events through conflict and consequences by showing what got in the way and how you overcame obstacles.",
  "Replace 'and then' between story beats with 'but' or 'therefore' to create tension, a principle learned from @kallaway shared from Trey and Matt (creators of South Park)."
 ],
 "content": "Storytelling is all the rageee in content atm, but how can you tell better stories? Here are three ways: 1) Get specific. Add little details that put us in the moment - what you saw/ heard/felt/smelt/tasted 2) Raise the stakes - What did you want/ what do you stand to lose? 2) Connect events through conflict and consequences. What got in the way, how did you overcome obstacles? (Seperate) Fun storytelling bit of the day: I learnt this principle from @kallaway who shared it from Trey & Matt (the creators of South Park): replace “and then” between story beats with “but” or “therefore” to create tension. Happy storytelling fam 🤸🤸🤸 I cooked dinner for my partner's parents and we all sat down and ate it together. It was the first time meeting my partner's parents and I made them lamb, but I burnt it so we ended up having pizza instead and it was fine. It was the first time meeting his parents. I had practiced this lamb five times, I was going to get it perfect and then just as the doorbell rang... so did the smoke alarm. I had completely charred the whole thing. Text shown on screen: Bad Storytelling, OK Storytelling, Great Storytelling.",
